      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "The growth of the Internet of Marine Things (IoMT) is quickly transforming work on the seas and in the ocean by connecting many devices. The convergence of diverse data types within this ecosystem gives rise to what is now termed Big Marine Data. Leaning on the data we collect is essential for developing new applications in marine science, environmental protection, shipping systems and defence. This article looks at how Big Data analytics and IoMT depend on each other, thanks to new technologies such as Artificial Intelligence, machine learning, deep learning, distributed computing architectures, digital twins and blockchain which offer unmatched knowledge and work improvements. The issue of key technical problems is considered, including significant communication limitations, a shortage of energy supplies, mismatched types of data and special threats to cybersecurity that are found only on the ocean. However, harnessing fully the potential of Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Big Data in maritime environments is affected by existing limitations of communication, lack of energy supply to the remote devices, data heterogeneity and unique cybersecurity risks in the marine environment. This survey aims to respond to the great need to overcome these limitations by synthesizing the present state of research and identifying potential future lines of research. Consequently, this survey serves as a watershed-like roadmap of sorts for academics, lawmakers and industry decision-makers as they try to navigate through the complexities and the opportunities across the intersection of Big Data and IoMT.",
      "abstract_not_available": false,
      "snippet": "The growth of the Internet of Marine ThingsInternet of Marine Things (IoMT) (IoMT) is quickly transforming work on the seas and in the ocean by...",
